<section class="fragment-plan" data-artifact="plan" data-slice="viewer-pitch" data-rev="0">
  <table class="files-touched pl-files">
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Status</th><th>Module</th><th>Lines</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">domain</span> crates/protocol/src/message.rs, lib.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">TeamRef gains team.kit.primary and team.kit.secondary as lower-case hex strings. The opening message is the only place a page can learn a club's colours, and today it carries an identifier and a name and nothing else, while the standing steering file needs the secondary colour for every marker ring. PROTOCOL_VERSION stays 1: two fields are added, no field changes meaning, no message is removed, and both producers are updated in the same commit.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>crates/protocol</td><td>+18 / -4</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">service</span> crates/stream/src/server.rs, replay.rs, lib.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">The hello builder reads the kit colours from the two loaded team files. The replayer forwards the stored hello verbatim instead of synthesising one, which retires a latent fault — the current code hard-codes keyframe_interval to the default rather than reading the fixture — and carries the kit colours through replay for free. The --sustain cap paces at min(speed, sustain) while the hello still announces the requested speed, so the page receives a stream slower than it asked for. One new error variant names a fixture whose first entry is not a hello.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>crates/stream</td><td>+80 / -24</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">service</span> crates/engine-cli/src/web.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">A std::net::TcpListener on 127.0.0.1:0 serving the page directory over plain HTTP, GET and HEAD only, with a MIME table that types .mjs correctly and refuses any path containing two dots. Every response carries Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp, which the chosen memory gauge requires and which no file:// page and no bare static server can supply. Emits web.serving with the directory, the file count, the isolation flag and the port.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>crates/engine-cli</td><td>+220</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">cli</span> crates/engine-cli/src/cli.rs, serve.rs, replay.rs, main.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">--web &lt;DIR&gt; on both serve and replay; --sustain &lt;f32&gt; on replay alone, with help text naming it a test harness for the viewer's lag notice so no later slice promotes it to a live match. Both commands start the static server before the socket and print the page address last, because that is the line a reader copies. Every help line stays inside 80 columns.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>crates/engine-cli</td><td>+54 / -6</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">ui</span> web/index.html, tokens.css, layout.css, fonts/fonts.css, components/match-control.css, four woff2 faces</summary><div class="pl-card">The page shell at 1280 by 800: a 56-pixel header, then three fixed columns of 336, 616 and 296 with an 8-pixel gutter, with the header and both side columns present and empty so the pitch lands at its shipping position and viewer-match-day fills regions rather than moving the pitch. All 36 --tl- tokens in one :root block, every value OKLCH, no pure black and no pure white. Barlow Condensed 700 and IBM Plex Sans 400/500/600 as local woff2, never a font service. The match-control primitive in four heights, five states and both themes, of which this slice uses two heights.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>web/</td><td>+450</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">domain</span> web/decode.mjs, interpolate.mjs, history.mjs, schedule.mjs, colour.mjs, stoppages.mjs, signal.mjs</summary><div class="pl-card">The seven pure modules, every one of them free of DOM, canvas and socket, so node --test proves all of them before a browser opens. The decoder reads a DataView at fixed little-endian offsets with no allocation per tick. The interpolation function clamps its fraction into zero-to-one and therefore cannot express a position outside the segment between two received ticks, which is RIM-5 as a type constraint rather than a comment. The history decodes to absolute Int16Array arrays at 24.2 MB against a 300 MB budget, twelvefold headroom bought for an O(1) rewind. The scheduler derives frame rate from timestamp deltas, never a callback count. The colour module clamps a kit hex away from the extremes before the 3:1 turf test, because a shipped team file carries pure black.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>web/ pure modules</td><td>+820</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">ui</span> web/pitch.mjs, mark.mjs</summary><div class="pl-card">The coordinate map from the engine's centred metre space onto 616 by 411 pixels, the markings, 22 markers with rings and shirt numbers, and the ball with a falling-alpha trail drawn as one path. Backing store scaled by devicePixelRatio, alpha off, integer coordinates, one fill-style change per team, static markings pre-rendered once and blitted. The touchline mark is one function taking a tile size, a radius of size times 0.21 and three tokens; no logo is ever loaded from an image file, and club crests later call the same function with kit colours in place of the turf.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>web/ rendering</td><td>+370</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">service</span> web/socket.mjs, playback.mjs, main.mjs</summary><div class="pl-card">The socket sets binaryType to arraybuffer, which avoids the asynchronous Blob step the default would force at 400 frames per second. Playback carries play and pause, the 1x to 8x selector, skip to the next stoppage over a merged restart-and-event index, the rewind scrubber, and the sustained-rate estimator — the browser exposes no inbound-queue depth, so measuring arrival against the requested speed is the only way the lag notice can exist at all. main.mjs exposes window.__touchline with the last rendered positions, the signal ring, the history gauge and the frame numbers; three acceptance criteria read that hook.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>web/ transport</td><td>+560</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">test</span> web/tests/ (helpers plus six test files), crates/engine-cli/tests/web_cli.rs, and three modified Rust test files</summary><div class="pl-card">Six node --test files with nothing installed and no package.json: the interpolation proof drives a fraction of 1.5 and of -0.5 directly; the scheduler proof runs a synthetic 8x stream and asserts the cursor never passes the newest received tick; the history proof asserts exactly 25,380,000 bytes for a full match. On the Rust side: the page address is printed, a missing directory is refused naming the path, a traversal path answers 403, a .mjs response carries text/javascript, both isolation headers ride on every response, a replay's hello is byte-identical to the recorded one, and --speed 8 --sustain 3 delivers at three times real time within 5 percent.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>tests</td><td>+900 / -0</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">docs</span> docs/reference/protocol.md, README.md, DESIGN.md</summary><div class="pl-card">The hello table gains the two kit fields and a new section documents the static server, its MIME table, and the two isolation headers with the constraint that require-corp refuses any cross-origin subresource. README gains the run instructions the shape's Documentation Plan requires. DESIGN.md gains the ten new token rows so it never disagrees with web/tokens.css. The document test parses these tables, so the reference cannot drift from the code.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>docs</td><td>+74 / -4</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="pl-risks">
    <aside class="callout callout-error"><div class="callout-hd">R1 · The refresh-rate false pass</div><div class="callout-body">requestAnimationFrame fires at the display's refresh rate, not at 60 Hz. On a 120 or 144 Hz panel a callback count reports 120 or 144 and a naive sixty-frames-per-second assertion passes for the wrong reason while telling nobody anything true. Derive frame rate from consecutive timestamp deltas and report refresh_hz beside every reading; verify records both numbers or the drive does not count.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-error"><div class="callout-hd">R2 · No browser has ever opened this socket</div><div class="callout-body">Three slices verified the protocol against a Rust client only, and the stream verify artifact says so at line 241. Step 12 is a deliberate ten-line page that connects, reads the hello and logs it, driven in the in-app browser before any rendering code exists to blame. Confirm there that require-corp does not refuse the four same-origin font files.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R3 · Interpolation that quietly extrapolates</div><div class="callout-body">This is RIM-5 and commitment C2: a viewer that invents movement between ticks satisfies the criterion in letter and breaks the promise in spirit. The mitigation is structural rather than a review note — the interpolation function clamps its fraction and cannot express a position outside the segment, and the scheduler moves the cursor rather than the fraction.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R4 · The sustain cap is a test harness only</div><div class="callout-body">It exists to make the lag-notice criterion drivable. Reachable from a real match it would silently throttle a live engine, so it sits on replay alone, never on serve, and its help text says what it is for.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R5 · The isolation headers restrict what the page may load later</div><div class="callout-body">require-corp refuses any cross-origin subresource. Everything this page loads is same-origin, so nothing is lost today, but a later slice wanting an external resource will hit it. web.serving reports the isolation flag so a run with the headers off is visible rather than silently ungauged.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R6 · The fixture must be re-recorded</div><div class="callout-body">From step 3 onward the stored hello is forwarded verbatim, so a fixture recorded before the kit fields exist carries no colours and the page would draw grey markers with no error anywhere. Step 7 re-records at seed 7, and the byte-identity test makes a stale fixture fail loudly rather than render wrongly.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R7 · History growth past ticks_expected</div><div class="callout-body">Stoppage time makes a match run longer than the opening message predicts, and a fixed buffer would either refuse or silently drop the closing minutes. The buffers double instead, and a test drives growth and asserts every earlier tick survives.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-info"><div class="callout-hd">R8 · Path traversal against the static server</div><div class="callout-body">The server serves an operator-named directory on loopback to a browser the operator opened, so the exposure is small. Refuse any path containing two dots with 403 anyway, serve GET and HEAD only, and answer anything else with 405.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-info"><div class="callout-hd">R9 · The memory gauge may never resolve</div><div class="callout-body">The browser's own memory measurement is asynchronous and may be coalesced or deferred, so the gauge can read null at the moment verify looks. Write null, never block a frame, and treat a null at verify as a failed drive rather than a pass. The exact history byte count sits beside it and is always available, which is why the product owner kept both numbers.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-info"><div class="callout-hd">R10 · viewer-event is a fifth record kind</div><div class="callout-body">The observability contract names four record kinds and this is not one of them. Emit it as an additive extra, exactly as content.hash and change.queue_id were, and let the observability audit settle whether it is a kind of its own.</div></aside>
  </div>

